  <scopecontent id="aspace_26ee0ea289be1315c59db7e1bb2b0659">
    <head>Content Description</head>
<p>Assignments created by students of Marina Zurkow, an instructor at NYU Abu Dhabi in Interactive Media and at the Tisch School of the Arts at New York University, from 2018-2019.  The course, based on the theme of "temporary experts," asked students to create field guides on topics they had studied or had personal experience of.  The accession consists of field guides created by her students at NYU Abu Dhabi in the spring semesters of 2018 and 2019, and her students in New York at NYU in fall 2018.</p>  </scopecontent>
  <bioghist id="aspace_ce48260fe38de824d2f15702cf58b667">
    <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
<p>Course Description, from 2018-2019 NYUAD Bulletin: 
IM-UH 1513 Temporary Expert: Developing a Research-based Art Practice 
Typically offered: spring 
Crosslisted with Art and Art History; Design </p><p>What does it mean to become a "temporary expert?" How does one develop one's own creative research-based practice? This course will address these questions by engaging with Abu Dhabi's environmental and social dimensions as a subject for research, context and imaginative art and design opportunities. Students will adopt a wide variety of tools and strategies in order to lay the foundations for a research-based art practice that considers materials, media, context, and audience, as well as one's personal strengths and desires. Over the course of the semester, students will develop art and design projects that interface with a multiplicity of other disciplines, and engage in idea exchange with experts in the field. Through hands-on practice, case studies, and readings on systems thinking, communication, and the idea of "the public," we will explore method, documentation and presentation of research, as well as the merits of both success and failure.</p>  </bioghist>
